A||N||4||BigTurbo87GT||Oil return ?||||1||
Z||000000||BigTurbo87GT||10-30-2000||05:16 PM||||Huy guys! I have enjoyed watching the forum for a couple of months. Now I'm starting to modify my GT and need draw on experience of those that have been where I'm going.<BR>I have a pretty large turbo (Holset H1C) that I need to plumb into my oil system. I have a pretty good Idea how I want to do it, but I am interested in how other people have handled the Oil return. The only way I can think of (as the oil has to fall away from the turbo) is to punch a whole in the Oil pan. I have heard of oil being returned through the dipstick housing but that would seem to be a little to restrictive, my return line is about a 9/16" OD pipe.<BR>In order to create this thru-pan fitting it would seem I would have to remove the oil pan to avoid contaminating oil with shavings.<BR>It would be great if there were some way to return oil that didn't involve having to take off the oil pan. At the same time, I'm not looking for a short cut, just alternatives.  Thanks a lot for any input, Bill<P>------------------<BR>87 GT, Clean stocker<BR>95 Z28, Clean not so stocker||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||10-30-2000||05:40 PM||||Well, you've waited long enough.  Now for some next-to-worthless  Stu input  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> :<P>Easiest way I can think of to return foamy oil to the crankcase, is through a valve cover.  A valve cover can be removed, modified and replaced (relatively) painlesssly.<P>Easier still, is to modify the oil filler cap itself.  Put a fitting through the oil filler cap (the caps available and cheap), and attach a flexible line to it, from the turbo's oil outlet.  That's one way...<P>Better yet, make an  oil-fill adapter "pipe" that sticks up out of the valve cover.  The adapter pipe would screw into the existing oil-fill hole in the valve cover, and the OE oil-fill cap would screw into the top end of the adapter pipe.  Kind of like the oil-filler pipes you used to see in older cars- they made it <B>so</B> easy to add oil, because you could stick the whole can upside down into the pipe!  So far so good  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">?  <P>Now you "tee" a fitting into the side of the new oil-filler pipe you just made, and plumb the turbo oil into <B>that</B>!       <P>BTW, as soon as you start making these oil-filler pipes,<B>I</B> want one, too <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> !!<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000002||BigTurbo87GT||10-30-2000||05:54 PM||||I wish it would be that easy ;-) Loved my old 283 Custom 100, huge tube for dumping oil into motor, course at the rate it used oil I needed it.<BR> I have a little knowledge of turbos and as far as I know the oil return point has to be lower than the turbo (gravity keeps any significant backpressure from developing on the return line and impeding oil flow through the turbo. The turbo supply line is usually tee'd off another line, I believe that's why the return line has to present as little-to-no backpressure as is possible. If there's too much pressure the oil will tend to take the other tee, and rob your turbo.||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||10-30-2000||06:30 PM||||Somehow I <B>knew</B> that was too easy!  Well, for crying out loud, why did't you say so?! And here I thought your turbo was going to be mounted on top of the roof of the car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">!<P>I apologize.  Was thinking back to my Turbo Corvair, which had the turbo oil drain back into the RHS valve cover... and all too often, an oil leak from the valve cover in that same area  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">   That thing had a big return line about the size you're talking about.  But of course, the turbo sat pretty high, compared to that little pancake 6-cylinder engine!   <P>I assume your turbo sits atop the bellhousing, where the exhaust crossover is ?||||reg||11
Z||000004||theogre||10-30-2000||06:46 PM||||The valve cover would be easier. It's generally desired for the oil to gravity drain back to the sump. That's allot of why the return line is usually larger than the supply line.<P>I'd return it to the pan. Installing the fitting with the pan off ensures a good seal.<P>Don't forget an oil accumulator on that. It saves cooking th bearing out of the turbo.||||reg||1
